{:edb/db-before-t 2052 :edb/db-after-t 2053 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535365 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:50:42.887Z" 13194139535365 true] [17592186229111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345998 13194139535365 true] [17592186229112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345977 13194139535365 true] [17592186229113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345984 13194139535365 true] [17592186229113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345991 13194139535365 true] [17592186229587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535365 true] [17592186229587 1100 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda =20$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.05$\n- $Δx=0.12$\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139535365 true] [17592186229587 1100 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $λ = 20$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $Δx = 0.05$\n2. $Δx = 0.12$\n3. $Δx = 0.2$\n4. $Δx = 0.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(λΔx) = 1 - λΔx$." 13194139535365 false] [17592186229587 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =20,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <20Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{20} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.05$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.12$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality.\n\nHence, Euler's method is unstable for the step sizes $Δx=0.12,$ $Δx=0.2,$ and $Δx=0.25.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345978 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1089 "q-333578" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1100 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1113 #uuid "9d4c3d13-fc7f-5983-b861-f2f35fe9addc" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345977 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535365 true] [17592186345978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345979 13194139535365 true] [17592186345978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345980 13194139535365 true] [17592186345978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345981 13194139535365 true] [17592186345978 1000 #edb/ref 1759218634