 Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345984 1113 #uuid "a0152c7d-3d41-54de-b8fe-02fb176c54ab" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345984 1145 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.2)| & =|1-0.2+\\frac{(0.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{12\\,281}{15\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.818…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.8)| & =|1-1.8+\\frac{(1.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1427}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.2854<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.7)| & =|1-2.7+\\frac{(2.7)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.7)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.7)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{70\\,307}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.878…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|1-6.6+\\frac{(6.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(6.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(6.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{236\\,627}{5000}| \\\\  & =47.3254>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345986 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345987 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345988 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345989 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345990 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1041 "selection" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345988 13194139535365 true] [17592186345985 1110 #uuid "de92090b-554b-571f-87ee-0f1b4942c263" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345986 1034 "I only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345986 1111 #uuid "6c33eb25-01c7-5bd1-ba37-b4e91f34281c" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345987 1034 "III only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345987 1111 #uuid "3d7c39fd-af7e-5c6a-ae02-3f446a8e886a" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345988 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345988 1111 #uuid "06c4ce89-5bee-5eb6-aa4b-c9ba9efbe0ba" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345989 1034 "I, II, III, and IV" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345989 1111 #uuid "151b9791-bf4e-5c53-8cb9-11ae903cef3f" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345990 1034 "II only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345990 1103 #e