d}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1-0.8/2}{1+0.8/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.6}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.4285…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|\\frac{1-2.9/2}{1+2.9/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.45}{2.45}| \\\\  & =0.1836…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6.2)| & =|\\frac{1-6.2/2}{1+6.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-2.1}{4.1}| \\\\  & =0.5121…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345993 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345994 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345995 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345996 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345997 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1041 "selection" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345997 13194139535365 true] [17592186345992 1110 #uuid "376ebb92-3a05-5a89-b283-4a3a95daa7ce" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345993 1034 "I only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345993 1111 #uuid "fa0d1520-8647-5430-92f9-b17a18492f7c" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345994 1034 "III only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345994 1111 #uuid "5b966832-f427-5a7e-8d74-5910d50b882e" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345995 1034 "None" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345995 1111 #uuid "ca920c94-b337-55cb-8e66-5b2176c8c6ee" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345996 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345996 1111 #uuid "f604303d-b36a-5a63-8607-b5facc05f176" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345997 1034 "I, II, III, and IV" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186345997 1111 #uuid "9cd33cd6-1488-59d5-8155-7c2a05769888" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345999 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1089 "q-334627" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1100 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =4$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.45$\n- $Δx=0.9$\n- $Δx=2.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1113 #uuid "154131ca-e8c5-5118-95ce-132cf053cf13" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345998 1145 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =4,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.45$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.9$ doesn't satisfy the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=2.2$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.2$ and $Δx=0.45.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346000 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346001 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346002 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346003 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346004 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1041 "selection" 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346003 13194139535365 true] [17592186345999 1110 #uuid "ee463d90-780a-51c1-bd97-d4ecdd0a4ec3" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346000 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186346000 1111 #uuid "ec809665-63eb-5f0a-9fd2-06b008852a87" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346001 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186346001 1111 #uuid "a082a765-a72c-5a31-b578-481f23db8de0" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346002 1034 "III only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186346002 1111 #uuid "aa2db007-a8ad-51a2-89be-3a28f1fbf73b" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346003 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186346003 1111 #uuid "54f9fb79-7640-5fd9-9193-482335b2919f" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346004 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535365 true] [17592186346004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535365 true] [17592186346004 1111 #uuid "3ab9f9c4-401b-52